            <div xml:id="d474">

                <pb facs="0479" n="469" sameAs="frus:frus1937v05/pg469"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">822.00 Revolutions/86: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Ecuador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Gonzalez</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Quito</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-10-24T16:00:00-05:00">October 24, 1937—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received October 25—9:15 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>62. Referring to my telegram No. 61, October 23, 9 a.m., <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >coup d’état</hi> consummated without any apparent opposition. Three army
                    officers have been appointed to the Cabinet including the previous Minister of
                    Finance who is acting as Foreign Minister. In view of the announced intention to
                    remove corrupt elements which was the ostensible reason for the movement, many
                    changes in Government officers are expected.</p>
                <p>Inasmuch as constitutional succession was disregarded the question of recognition
                    arises upon which I shall report fully.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gonzalez</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
